{
  "title": "HPA vs KEDA vs Karpenter: autoscaling traps",
  "content": "The tutorials all show the happy path. The kubernetes autoscaling advice that circulates internally rarely matches what's in the public docs. This works through the problem properly, from cause to fix.\n\n## The problem this solves\n\nYou have a Kubernetes cluster. Some workloads are steady HTTP APIs. Some are queue consumers that sit idle until a burst of jobs arrives. Some are batch jobs that need a GPU for twenty minutes and then vanish. You have three autoscalers available — HorizontalPodAutoscaler (HPA), KEDA, and Karpenter — and they overlap in ways that are not obvious from their names. HPA scales pods based on CPU or memory. KEDA scales pods based on external events (queue depth, Kafka lag, cron schedules). Karpenter scales *nodes* by provisioning EC2 instances directly, bypassing the traditional Cluster Autoscaler and node-group model. The trap is assuming they are interchangeable, or that stacking all three without thought produces a system that scales correctly. It usually produces a system that scales too late, too aggressively, or not at all, and the failure mode is invisible until traffic arrives.\n\nThis tutorial is a decision framework plus working configuration for each tool, with the specific edge cases that cause production incidents: HPA's missing-metrics behavior, KEDA's scale-to-zero cold-start cost, and Karpenter's interaction with pod disruption budgets and spot interruptions. By the end you should be able to look at a workload and know which autoscaler owns it.\n\n## The three tools, and what each actually controls\n\nBefore any YAML, get the mental model right. These tools operate at different layers.\n\n**HPA** is a built-in Kubernetes controller. It reads metrics from the metrics-server (or a custom metrics API) and adjusts the `replicas` field on a Deployment, ReplicaSet, or StatefulSet. It does not create nodes. If the cluster has no schedulable capacity, HPA will scale the replica count up and the new pods will sit `Pending`.\n\n**KEDA** is a Kubernetes Operator that installs a `ScaledObject` custom resource. KEDA itself does not replace HPA — it creates and manages an HPA object under the hood, feeding it metrics from external sources via the Kubernetes external metrics API. When a `ScaledObject` targets zero replicas, KEDA activates the workload from zero and then hands control to the HPA it manages. KEDA also supports scaling to zero, which plain HPA cannot do.\n\n**Karpenter** is a node autoscaler. It watches for unschedulable pods and provisions EC2 instances (or other cloud provider instances) that match the pod's resource requests, node selectors, and tolerations. It also consolidates underutilized nodes. Karpenter does not scale pods; it makes sure pods have somewhere to land.\n\nSo the layers are: KEDA or HPA decide *how many pods*. Karpenter decides *how many nodes and of what shape*. The common mistake is treating Karpenter as a replacement for HPA — it is not. A Deployment with a fixed replica count of 3 will stay at 3 pods no matter how many nodes Karpenter provisions.\n\n| Tool | Scales | Metric source | Scale to zero | Node awareness |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| HPA | Pod replicas | CPU, memory, custom/external metrics | No (minReplicas ≥ 1) | No |\n| KEDA | Pod replicas (via managed HPA) | 50+ scalers: SQS, Kafka, Prometheus, cron, etc. | Yes | No |\n| Karpenter | Nodes (EC2 instances) | Pod resource requests, node selectors | N/A | Yes |\n\n## Prerequisites and what you'll build\n\nYou need a Kubernetes cluster (1.24+ for Karpenter's current API; check the Karpenter docs for the exact supported version matrix for your provider). You need `kubectl` and `helm`. For KEDA you need the KEDA Helm chart. For Karpenter you need the Karpenter Helm chart and IAM permissions for the controller to create instances — the exact policy differs by cloud provider, so follow the official Karpenter getting-started guide for your provider rather than copying a policy from a blog post.\n\nWe will build three workloads that represent the three shapes:\n\n1. A stateless HTTP API scaled by CPU with HPA — the boring case, done correctly.\n2. A queue consumer scaled by SQS depth with KEDA, including scale-to-zero.\n3. A batch job that requests a GPU, scheduled onto a node that Karpenter provisions on demand.\n\n## Step 1 — Set up the environment and verify the metrics pipeline\n\nHPA and KEDA both depend on a working metrics pipeline. If `kubectl top pods` returns an error, HPA will not scale and the reason will be buried in events. Verify first.\n\n```bash\n# metrics-server is required for CPU/memory HPA\nkubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/components.yaml\nkubectl top nodes\nkubectl top pods -A | head\n```\n\nIf `kubectl top` fails, check that metrics-server pods are running and that the API service `v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io` is available:\n\n```bash\nkubectl get apiservice v1beta1.metrics.k8s.io -o yaml\n```\n\nA common failure is metrics-server unable to scrape kubelets because of TLS certificate issues — the fix is provider-specific (adding `--kubelet-insecure-tls` is a workaround, not a production fix). Do not proceed to HPA until `kubectl top pods` works.\n\nFor KEDA, install the operator:\n\n```bash\nhelm repo add kedacore https://kedacore.github.io/charts\nhelm repo update\nhelm install keda kedacore/keda --namespace keda --create-namespace\nkubectl get pods -n keda\n```\n\nFor Karpenter, install the controller per the official guide for your provider. The controller needs a node IAM role and a controller IAM role; the exact names and policies are documented per provider. Do not guess them.\n\n## Step 2 — HPA for steady HTTP workloads\n\nHPA is the right tool when traffic correlates with a resource you can measure — CPU, memory, or a custom metric your app exports. The default behavior scales on average CPU utilization across pods.\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: autoscaling/v2\nkind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler\nmetadata:\n  name: api-hpa\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    apiVersion: apps/v1\n    kind: Deployment\n    name: api\n  minReplicas: 2\n  maxReplicas: 20\n  metrics:\n    - type: Resource\n      resource:\n        name: cpu\n        target:\n          type: Utilization\n          averageUtilization: 60\n  behavior:\n    scaleUp:\n      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 0\n      policies:\n        - type: Percent\n          value: 100\n          periodSeconds: 15\n    scaleDown:\n      stabilizationWindowSeconds: 300\n      policies:\n        - type: Percent\n          value: 25\n          periodSeconds: 60\n```\n\nThe `behavior` block is where most production HPA configs go wrong. By default, HPA scales down after a 5-minute stabilization window, which is fine. But scale-up can be sluggish if you leave the default 0-second window combined with a low percentage policy — the controller may only add one pod per evaluation cycle. The config above allows doubling every 15 seconds on the way up and removes at most 25% of pods per minute on the way down. The asymmetry is deliberate: scale up fast, scale down slow, because a premature scale-down followed by a traffic spike causes a thundering herd on the remaining pods.\n\n**Gotcha: HPA and missing metrics.** When the metrics API returns no data for a pod — for example, a pod that just started and has not been scraped yet — HPA treats that pod's utilization as unknown. If enough pods are unknown, HPA cannot compute a ratio and does not scale. The event on the HPA object reads `failed to get cpu utilization: missing request for cpu`. The fix is to set resource *requests* on every container. HPA computes utilization as a percentage of the request, not of the limit. A container with no CPU request is invisible to HPA.\n\n**Gotcha: HPA with memory.** Memory-based HPA is a trap for most workloads. Memory usage rarely drops when load drops, because runtimes hold onto heap. An HPA targeting 70% memory will scale up and then never scale down. Use CPU or a custom request-rate metric for HTTP services.\n\nFor a custom metric, expose something like requests-per-second via Prometheus and use the Prometheus Adapter to register it as a custom metric. That is more work but produces scaling that tracks traffic rather than CPU, which is often a poor proxy for a service that is I/O-bound.\n\n## Step 3 — KEDA for event-driven and scale-to-zero workloads\n\nKEDA's value is twofold: it can read metrics HPA cannot (queue depth, Kafka consumer lag, cron schedules), and it can scale a Deployment to zero replicas when there is no work. That second property is what makes it appropriate for queue consumers that are idle most of the day.\n\nHere is a `ScaledObject` for an SQS consumer:\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: keda.sh/v1alpha1\nkind: ScaledObject\nmetadata:\n  name: sqs-consumer\nspec:\n  scaleTargetRef:\n    name: sqs-consumer\n  minReplicaCount: 0\n  maxReplicaCount: 30\n  pollingInterval: 15\n  cooldownPeriod: 120\n  triggers:\n    - type: aws-sqs-queue\n      metadata:\n        queueURL: https://sqs.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/123456789012/jobs\n        queueLength: \"10\"\n        awsRegion: us-east-1\n        identityOwner: pod\n```\n\n`queueLength: \"10\"` means KEDA targets one replica per 10 messages in the queue. With 100 messages visible, it asks for 10 replicas. `pollingInterval: 15` means it checks SQS every 15 seconds. `cooldownPeriod: 120` means after the last message is consumed, it waits two minutes before scaling to zero.\n\nThose three numbers are where the tuning happens. A short polling interval reacts faster but costs more SQS API calls and more load on the scaler. A long cooldown avoids flapping when the queue drains and refills, but keeps idle pods running longer. For a queue that bursts every few minutes, a cooldown of 120–300 seconds is reasonable. For a queue that gets one message per hour, a shorter cooldown saves money.\n\n**Gotcha: scale-to-zero cold start.** When KEDA scales a Deployment from 0 to 1, the new pod must schedule, pull its image, start the process, and connect to the queue. That is not instant. If the queue receives a burst and your consumer takes 30 seconds to become ready, messages sit in the queue for 30 seconds. For latency-sensitive queues, set `minReplicaCount: 1` and accept the cost of one warm pod. Scale-to-zero is for workloads where a cold start is acceptable — batch processing, nightly jobs, low-priority consumers.\n\n**Gotcha: KEDA and HPA conflict.** KEDA creates an HPA object named `keda-hpa-<scaledobject-name>`. If you also create your own HPA targeting the same Deployment, the two fight. Do not mix them on the same workload. Pick one.\n\nKEDA also supports cron triggers, which is useful for predictable traffic:\n\n```yaml\n  triggers:\n    - type: cron\n      metadata:\n        timezone: UTC\n        start: \"0 8 * * *\"\n        end: \"0 20 * * *\"\n        desiredReplicas: \"5\"\n```\n\nThis scales to 5 replicas during business hours and back down outside them, regardless of load. It is a blunt instrument, but for a service with a known daily pattern it is more reliable than waiting for CPU to rise.\n\n## Step 4 — Karpenter for node provisioning\n\nKarpenter's job is to make sure pods have nodes to run on, and to remove nodes that are no longer needed. It does this by watching for unschedulable pods and provisioning instances that satisfy their requirements.\n\nA minimal `NodePool` and `EC2NodeClass` (AWS-specific; other providers have equivalents):\n\n```yaml\napiVersion: karpenter.sh/v1\nkind: NodePool\nmetadata:\n  name: default\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      requirements:\n        - key: kubernetes.io/arch\n          operator: In\n          values: [\"amd64\"]\n        - key: karpenter.sh/capacity-type\n          operator: In\n          values: [\"on-demand\", \"spot\"]\n        - key: node.kubernetes.io/instance-type\n          operator: In\n          values: [\"m5.large\", \"m5.xlarge\", \"m5.2xlarge\"]\n  limits:\n    cpu: \"1000\"\n  disruption:\n    consolidationPolicy: WhenEmptyOrUnderutilized\n    consolidateAfter: 30s\n---\napiVersion: karpenter.k8s.aws/v1\nkind: EC2NodeClass\nmetadata:\n  name: default\nspec:\n  amiFamily: AL2\n  role: KarpenterNodeRole\n  subnetSelectorTerms:\n    - tags:\n        karpenter.sh/discovery: my-cluster\n  securityGroupSelectorTerms:\n    - tags:\n        karpenter.sh/discovery: my-cluster\n```\n\nThe `requirements` block constrains what Karpenter is allowed to provision. Allowing both `on-demand` and `spot` lets Karpenter choose spot when available, which is cheaper but interruptible. The `consolidationPolicy` tells Karpenter to remove nodes that are empty or underutilized — this is how you avoid paying for nodes that HPA scaled down.\n\n**Gotcha: Karpenter and pod disruption budgets.** When Karpenter consolidates a node, it evicts pods. If a pod's PodDisruptionBudget (PDB) does not allow eviction, the node stays. A common pattern is a PDB with `minAvailable: 1` on a Deployment that Karpenter wants to consolidate from 2 pods to 1 — the eviction is blocked and the node lingers. Check `kubectl get pdb -A` and the Karpenter logs when consolidation seems stuck.\n\n**Gotcha: Karpenter and spot interruptions.** Spot instances can be reclaimed with a two-minute warning. Karpenter handles the interruption notice by cordoning and draining the node, but your pods need to tolerate that. For stateful workloads or long-running batch jobs, spot is a poor fit unless the job can checkpoint. For stateless services with a PDB and multiple replicas, spot is usually fine.\n\n**Gotcha: Karpenter does not scale pods.** This is worth repeating because it is the most common misconception. If you deploy a GPU batch job with `replicas: 1`, Karpenter will provision one GPU node. If you want ten parallel batch workers, you need either a fixed replica count of 10 or an HPA/KEDA scaling on a queue. Karpenter only responds to what the scheduler cannot place.\n\n## How the three interact in practice\n\nA typical production setup uses all three, each at its layer:\n\n- HPA scales the HTTP API between 2 and 20 pods based on CPU.\n- KEDA scales the queue consumer between 0 and 30 pods based on SQS depth.\n- Karpenter provisions nodes when either of the above creates pods that do not fit, and consolidates when they scale down.\n\nThe interaction to watch is timing. HPA reacts in seconds to a minute. KEDA's polling interval adds up to 15 seconds of lag. Karpenter's node provisioning takes minutes — instance launch, kubelet registration, image pull. So the end-to-end time from traffic spike to a new pod serving requests is dominated by Karpenter, not HPA. If your HPA scales to 20 pods but the cluster only has capacity for 5, the other 15 sit Pending until Karpenter catches up. That is why `minReplicas` and node headroom matter: keep enough spare capacity to absorb the first minute of a spike, and let Karpenter handle the sustained load.\n\nA useful pattern is to set HPA `minReplicas` to a value that fits on the existing nodes, and let Karpenter handle anything above that. If HPA's minimum is 2 and the nodes can hold 6 pods, a spike to 6 is instant; a spike to 20 triggers Karpenter.\n\n## Verification checklist\n\nAfter configuring all three, verify each layer independently:\n\n1. **Metrics:** `kubectl top pods` returns data for every pod you expect to scale.\n2. **HPA:** `kubectl describe hpa api-hpa` shows current metrics and no `failed to get` events.\n3. **KEDA:** `kubectl get scaledobject` shows `READY=True`, and `kubectl get hpa` shows a `keda-hpa-*` object for each ScaledObject.\n4. **Karpenter:** `kubectl get nodepool` shows `READY=True`, and `kubectl logs -n karpenter -l app.kubernetes.io/name=karpenter` shows no provisioning errors.\n5. **End-to-end:** generate load against the API and watch `kubectl get pods -w` — you should see HPA add pods and Karpenter add nodes if needed. Send a burst of messages to the queue and watch the consumer scale from zero.\n\nIf HPA does not scale, check events on the HPA object first. If KEDA does not scale, check the ScaledObject status and the KEDA operator logs. If Karpenter does not provision, check for unschedulable pods with `kubectl get pods -A --field-selector=status.phase=Pending` and read the events.\n\n## Frequently Asked Questions\n\n**Can I use HPA and KEDA together on the same Deployment?**\nNo — KEDA creates and manages its own HPA object for each ScaledObject. If you also create a standalone HPA targeting the same Deployment, the two controllers will fight over the replica count. Pick one. If you need both CPU-based and queue-based scaling, use KEDA with multiple triggers, since KEDA supports a CPU trigger alongside external triggers.\n\n**Does Karpenter replace the Cluster Autoscaler?**\nYes, in the sense that you run one or the other, not both. Karpenter provisions nodes directly rather than through Auto Scaling Groups, which lets it choose instance types based on pod requirements rather than predefined node groups. Running both against the same cluster causes conflicting node lifecycle decisions.\n\n**Why does my HPA show \"unknown\" for current metrics?**\nThe most common cause is missing CPU requests on the container. HPA computes utilization as a percentage of the request, so a container without a request has no denominator. The second most common cause is metrics-server not scraping the pod yet — new pods take a scrape interval to appear. Check `kubectl describe hpa` for the specific event.\n\n**How do I scale a GPU batch job that runs occasionally?**\nUse KEDA with a queue trigger (SQS, RabbitMQ, or a cron trigger) to scale the job Deployment from zero, and Karpenter with a NodePool that allows GPU instance types. The pod template must request `nvidia.com/gpu: 1` so Karpenter knows to provision a GPU node. Set `minReplicaCount: 0` so the job costs nothing when idle, and accept the cold-start time for the node and image pull.\n\n## Where to go from here\n\nStart with the workload you understand least: open your cluster's HPA objects and check whether every container in the target Deployments has a CPU request set. That single check catches the most common autoscaling failure before it reaches production. Run `kubectl get hpa -A -o yaml | grep -A2 'target:'` and look for any container missing a `requests.cpu` value — if you find one, add it and watch the HPA's behavior change on the next load test.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
